05:29.240 --> 05:32.620
But I follow very strict risk management rules.

05:33.440 --> 05:39.060
That's something that I learned from, you know, first Jason was the first person to really hammer that in my head.

05:39.720 --> 05:44.240
I risk, you know, about 1% of my total trading capital per trade.

05:44.240 --> 05:50.120
Sometimes it's less, sometimes it's more, depending on the type of trade and the confidence I have in it.

05:50.580 --> 05:54.140
But, you know, in general, I don't stray far from 1%.

05:54.140 --> 05:57.940
And there's multiple reasons why I do that.

05:57.940 --> 06:03.780
Number one is it simplifies one of the decisions that you have to make when trading.

06:04.320 --> 06:06.920
So trading is a lot of decision making.

06:07.840 --> 06:14.840
And the more, you know, clear cut your decision process is, I think the easier it makes it.

06:14.840 --> 06:19.580
So I know that I'm going to be risking about 1% per trade.

06:20.500 --> 06:25.020
So I don't have to stress about deciding how much I put on my trade.

06:26.060 --> 06:35.740
And I enter trades with very clear invalidations as well, which is how I risk the amount that I'm comfortable with.

06:36.140 --> 06:39.420
You know, I know where my stop loss is relative to my entry.

06:39.420 --> 06:41.980
So I know exactly how much to put on a trade.

06:43.500 --> 06:45.500
So that's one main reason.

06:45.900 --> 06:51.740
Do you ever, like, increase risk in certain scenarios and decrease risk in certain scenarios?

06:51.880 --> 06:59.560
Or do you always do, like, a static 1% total, like, total loss kind of setup with your trades?

06:59.560 --> 07:03.860
Uh, so it depends on the trade setup.

07:04.020 --> 07:08.420
Like, um, sometimes I'll start to scale into trades with less than 1%.

07:08.420 --> 07:11.140
I'll start to scale in with, like, half a percent.

07:12.020 --> 07:18.400
Um, but if, if it's, like, a higher confidence swing trade, then I'll, I'll enter with, you know, sometimes 2%.

07:18.400 --> 07:22.100
